UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 20, Lesson 1: Kindness in our community', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Kindness is thinking about other people''s needs and acting to help. Acts of kindness in a community include helping a neighbour, welcoming a newcomer, thanking workers, tidying shared spaces and sharing time or food with people in need. Kind actions work best when they are safe, respect the other person''s wishes and do not expect anything back. Small acts can make a big difference.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite one safe act of kindness for a lonely neighbour and one way to ask first so you respect their wishes.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAct: draw a card and have an adult deliver it. Asking: ''Would you like a card, or a visit, from me?'' Respecting wishes makes the kindness welcome, and an adult keeps it safe.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking kindness has to cost money or be big. Words, time and small actions are kind too.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan a one-week kindness project for your community with five actions (home, school, neighbourhood). For each, name who it helps, how it is safe and who would help you. Do at least two and record how people responded.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Kindness is thinking about other people''s needs and acting to help. Acts of kindness in a community include helping a neighbour, welcoming a newcomer, thanking workers, tidying shared spaces and sharing time or food with people in need. Kind actions work best when they are safe, respect the other person''s wishes and do not expect anything back. Small acts can make a big difference.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-RRS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan a one-week kindness project for your community with five actions (home, school, neighbourhood). For each, name who it helps, how it is safe and who would help you. Do at least two and record how people responded. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example: Kia writes a kindness plan. Thank the bus driver (safe: say it while getting off). Collect litter in the park with an adult (safe: wear gloves). Draw cards for an aged care home (safe: an adult delivers them). Effect: three people feel appreciated. She asks ''Would you like help?'' before carrying a neighbour''s bags.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking kindness has to cost money or be big. Words, time and small actions are kind too. Correction: Act: draw a card and have an adult deliver it. Asking: ''Would you like a card, or a visit, from me?'' Respecting wishes makes the kindness welcome, and an adult keeps it safe.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking kindness has to cost money or be big. Words, time and small actions are kind too. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Kindness is thinking about other people''s needs and acting to help. Acts of kindness in a community include helping a neighbour, welcoming a newcomer, thanking workers, tidying shared spaces and sharing time or food with people in need. Kind actions work best when they are safe, respect the other person''s wishes and do not expect anything back. Small acts can make a big difference. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Kindness is thinking about other people''s needs and acting to help. Acts of kindness in a community include helping a neighbour, welcoming a newcomer, thanking workers, tidying shared spaces and sharing time or food with people in need. Kind actions work best when they are safe, respect the other person''s wishes and do not expect anything back. Small acts can make a big difference.","Thinking kindness has to cost money or be big. Words, time and small actions are kind too.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and demonstrate\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the reasoning\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking kindness has to cost money or be big. Words, time and small actions are kind too.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Leave someone out because the game felt easier that way."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation.","Leave someone out because the game felt easier that way.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new situation\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Act: draw a card and have an adult deliver it. Asking: ''Would you like a card, or a visit, from me?'' Respecting wishes makes the kindness welcome, and an adult keeps it safe. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["Act: draw a card and have an adult deliver it. Asking: ''Would you like a card, or a visit, from me?'' Respecting wishes makes the kindness welcome, and an adult keeps it safe.","Thinking kindness has to cost money or be big. Words, time and small actions are kind too.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Write one safe act of kindness for a lonely neighbour and one way to ask first s\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\". Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["The most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Act: draw a card and have an adult deliver it. Asking: ''Would you like a card, or a visit, from me?'' Respecting wishes makes the kindness welcome, and an adult keeps it safe. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["Act: draw a card and have an adult deliver it. Asking: ''Would you like a card, or a visit, from me?'' Respecting wishes makes the kindness welcome, and an adult keeps it safe.","Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking kindness has to cost money or be big. Words, time and small actions are kind too.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["The new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a sh\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named cues, rules or choices.","question":"Describe or practise a fresh situation that uses the central skill. Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Example: Kia writes a kindness plan. Thank the bus driver (safe: say it while getting off). Collect litter in the park with an adult (safe: wear gloves). Draw cards for an aged care home (safe: an adult delivers them). Effect: three people feel appreciated. She asks ''Would you like help?'' before carrying a neighbour''s bags.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the choice or strategy, not just make the answer longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus a corrected choice and a reason linked to safety, health or respect.","question":"Name one likely misconception or unsafe choice for this topic, correct it and say why the correction is safer, healthier or more respectful.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking kindness has to cost money or be big. Words, time and small actions are kind too. Correction: Act: draw a card and have an adult deliver it. Asking: ''Would you like a card, or a visit, from me?'' Respecting wishes makes the kindness welcome, and an adult keeps it safe.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w20-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise kindness.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use kindness with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify community and explain why it matters.","I can practise a safe act independently on a new prompt.","I can notice asking first needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Kindness is thinking about other people''s needs and acting to help. Acts of kindness in a community include helping a neighbour, welcoming a newcomer, thanking workers, tidying shared spaces and sharing time or food with people in need. Kind actions work best when they are safe, respect the other person''s wishes and do not expect anything back. Small acts can make a big difference. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Kindness is thinking about other people''s needs and acting to help. Acts of kindness in a community include helping a neighbour, welcoming a newcomer, thanking workers, tidying shared spaces and sharing time or food with people in need. Kind actions work best when they are safe, respect the other person''s wishes and do not expect anything back. Small acts can make a big difference.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Kia writes a kindness plan. Thank the bus driver (safe: say it while getting off). Collect litter in the park with an adult (safe: wear gloves). Draw cards for an aged care home (safe: an adult delivers them). Effect: three people feel appreciated. She asks ''Would you like help?'' before carrying a neighbour''s bags.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite one safe act of kindness for a lonely neighbour and one way to ask first so you respect their wishes.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAct: draw a card and have an adult deliver it. Asking: ''Would you like a card, or a visit, from me?'' Respecting wishes makes the kindness welcome, and an adult keeps it safe.","explain":"Kindness is thinking about other people''s needs and acting to help. Acts of kindness in a community include helping a neighbour, welcoming a newcomer, thanking workers, tidying shared spaces and sharing time or food with people in need. Kind actions work best when they are safe, respect the other person''s wishes and do not expect anything back. Small acts can make a big difference.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking kindness has to cost money or be big. Words, time and small actions are kind too. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Kindness that is safe.","caption":"Ask before helping.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Ask first</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Would you like a card?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safe</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">an adult deliver