helpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking kindness has to cost money or be big. Words, time and small actions are kind too. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Kia writes a kindness plan. Thank the bus driver (safe: say it while getting off). Collect litter in the park with an adult (safe: wear gloves). Draw cards for an aged care home (safe: an adult delivers them). Effect: three people feel appreciated. She asks ''Would you like help?'' before carrying a neighbour''s bags.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAct: draw a card and have an adult deliver it. Asking: ''Would you like a card, or a visit, from me?'' Respecting wishes makes the kindness welcome, and an adult keeps it safe.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking kindness has to cost money or be big. Words, time and small actions are kind too. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nKindness is thinking about other people''s needs and acting to help. Acts of kindness in a community include helping a neighbour, welcoming a newcomer, thanking workers, tidying shared spaces and sharing time or food with people in need. Kind actions work best when they are safe, respect the other person''s wishes and do not expect anything back. Small acts can make a big difference. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 20, Lesson 1: Kindness in our community","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Friendships and respect","week":20,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Kia writes a kindness plan. Thank the bus driver (safe: say it while getting off). Collect litter in the park with an adult (safe: wear gloves). Draw cards for an aged care home (safe: an adult delivers them). Effect: three people feel appreciated. She asks ''Would you like help?'' before carrying a neighbour''s bags.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Kindness that is safe.","caption":"Ask before helping.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Ask first</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Would you like a card?</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safe</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">an adult delivers it</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Community kindness acts.","captio